+---
+
+### 7.5 Ambient lights
+
+#### What the Ambient checkbox used to do
+
+On the stock path, ticking **Ambient** on a point, projector or area light turns it into a
+*multiplier*, not a light. In the deferred shader it runs after the environment probes and before
+the sun and every local light, and it does this:
+
+```
+indirect *= lerp( 1, colour, reach )
+```
+
+Three things follow, and they are the reason ambient lights feel unpredictable:
+
+* the number is **dimensionless**. `2.0` means "double the indirect light where I reach", `0.4`
+  means "cut it to 40 %";
+* there are two different models inside one checkbox, and the engine picks between them at
+  **1.0 per channel** — above it a brightening model that uses the surface normal and the SSDO bent
+  normal, below it a flat darkening model. A colour like `(1.4, 1.0, 0.7)` takes both;
+* **it cannot light a dark corner.** Anything times zero is zero. An ambient light only ever
+  scales light some probe already put on the surface, which is why a room with no probe coverage
+  stays black no matter how high the multiplier goes.
+
+**Cast Shadows** on an ambient light did nothing at all on the stock path — the tiled shader
+refuses to read an ambient light's shadow map — and it crashed the engine (see below).
+
+#### What it does under Scene Referred
+
+An ambient light entity becomes a **fill light**: an ordinary, additive, physically-lit source, on
+exactly the same footing as a point light of the same shape.
+
+| | stock | Scene Referred |
+|---|---|---|
+| operation | multiplies the indirect light | **adds** radiance, through the BRDF |
+| the number | dimensionless multiplier | **the point light's unit**: `Diffuse Multiplier` × `Color` in `10⁴/(1+r)²` cd — 1.0 ≈ 12 100 cd at the default 0.1 m bulb (§7.1, §7.2) |
+| in a dark corner | no effect | **fills it** |
+| exposure | follows whatever it multiplies | exposed exactly **once**, like every other light: it follows the aperture |
+| falloff / bulb size | ignored the bulb round trip | inverse square from the bulb surface, same as a point light |
+| Cast Shadows | ignored, and crashed | **works** |
+| volumetric fog | excluded | included |
+| projector / area shape | legacy ambient models | the normal projector cone and the normal LTC area light |
+
+This follows the trodden path rather than inventing one: Frostbite, Unreal and Unity HDRP have no
+ambient light entity at all — an environment probe is the ambient, and fill is a real lamp with a
+scrim on it. Under Scene Referred, so is ours.
+
+#### How to use one as fill
+
+Treat it as a soft lamp, not as a slider:
+
+1. Put it where the fill would physically stand (bounce card, window, practical), not at the room's
+   centre.
+2. Give it a number from §7.2's table. A fill is usually **1.5 to 3 stops under the key**, so if
+   the key is a `1.45` HMI, start the fill around `0.2 – 0.5`.
+3. Set a real **Radius** — it is now the light's reach, and `e_LightIlluminanceThreshold` still cuts
+   at 40 lux (§7.4).
+4. Raise **Attenuation Bulb Size** to soften it. That is the fill's "size", and it is also what
+   softens its shadow.
+5. Tick **Cast Shadows** if you want the fill to respect geometry. It is free of the old crash and
+   it behaves like any other shadow-casting light.
+
+For genuinely *global* ambient — the thing the checkbox was being used to fake — use an environment
+probe (§4) or the sky; that is what carries ambient light in this pipeline.
+
+#### What changes in an existing level
+
+The same authored number means something different on the two paths, exactly like the −1.65 stops
+on point lights and the area-light rework in §7.3. An ambient light left at `1.0` becomes a
+12 100 cd lamp; one left at `0.4` (a darkener) becomes a dim but *additive* lamp and no longer
+darkens anything. **A level lit with ambient lights has to be re-balanced when the switch goes on**,
+starting from §7.2's numbers. The stock path is untouched, byte for byte.
+
+#### The Ambient + Cast Shadows crash
+
+Ticking both **Ambient** and **Cast Shadows** on one light crashed the engine. It is a stock
+CRYENGINE 5.7 bug and it is fixed on both paths.
+
+The engine built a shadow map and a shadow render view for the light, but its shadow list only
+looks at ordinary and dynamic lights, never at ambient ones — so the view was created, filled, and
+then never closed. The next frame released it while it was still being written to, skipping the
+waits that make sure the shadow jobs have finished, and the renderer read memory that had just been
+freed. On the stock path the fix is simply that an ambient light no longer asks for a shadow map
+(nothing ever read it, so no picture changes; the leak and the wasted shadow passes go away). Under
+Scene Referred the light is a real light, so the checkbox now does what it says.
 * `e_LightIlluminanceThreshold` still cuts a light off at **40 lux**, which is generous for a
   daylight scene and far too aggressive for a night one. It is a cvar; lower it for night work.
 
